+`popups.js` also guards top-document same-tab navigation on the existing selected-site scopes. It uses trusted, cancellable Navigation API events; cross-origin URLs can be cancelled even when `canIntercept` is false. Ordinary native link/form navigation, exact link destinations captured before page handlers, same-host/parent/child scopes, reloads and history traversal remain usable. Link intent expires at the next task so it does not authorize a later unrelated redirect. A true `userActivation` flag alone is insufficient: a play button can activate an ad redirect. No new permission or setting is required, and disabling the existing guard still requires a page reload to remove installed hooks.
+
+The [Navigation API specification](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/dev/nav-history-apis.html) explains why cross-origin frame-initiated top navigation and browser UI navigation do not expose the same cancellable events. `sourceElement` arrived in [Chromium 135](https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/135); older supported engines use the native `userInitiated` flag while retaining exact captured-link checks. The fixture removes this newer property to exercise fallback behavior with real native navigation; it does not claim an actual old-browser run. `tests/same-tab-redirects-browser.cjs` records frame and HTTP-redirect bypasses separately as limitations, not successful protection assertions. Visible links whose actual destination is unwanted, restricted injection and page-world tampering are also outside this hook's guarantee. Keep tests on owned loopback pages behind a deny proxy; never use live suspicious destinations to demonstrate coverage.
